---
// /copyright/: takedowns for copyright and for private information, counter-notices and restoration,
// public redacted notices (night phase 16, E4 and E5; docs/MODERATION.md "Takedowns"). A draft for the
// owner's review. Static; science.css only.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import PolicyDraft from "../components/PolicyDraft.astro";
import { SITE_NAME } from "../config";
---
<Base
  title="Takedowns"
  description="How to have something removed for copyright or private information, how to answer with a counter-notice, and what is published."
  breadcrumb={[{ text: "Takedowns" }]}
>
  <div class="policy">
    <h1>Takedowns: copyright and private information</h1>
    <PolicyDraft drafted="2026-09-29" />
    <p class="summary">
      Where to ask depends on where the thing lives. {SITE_NAME} holds some things itself; the code of the
      GitHub side lives on GitHub.
    </p>

    <h2 id="held">1. What {SITE_NAME} holds</h2>
    <dl>
      <dt>A paper's record, the copies of its authors' scripts, one repository's or one file's copies, its tracing map</dt>
      <dd>Ask on <a href="/removal/">the removal page</a>, from the paper's page (“Request removal”): say who you are (an author, the rights holder, a person named), what to remove and why, with evidence.</dd>
      <dt>What people wrote in {SITE_NAME}: research issues and comments, profiles, star lists, commit statuses</dt>
      <dd>Use the “Report” link beside it, and choose “a copyright or licence infringement” or “private information”. A copyright notice needs an account (so that you can be answered in the site, without email), and says which work of yours it copies and where it is.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h2 id="github">2. Code on GitHub</h2>
    <p>
      A repository shown by {SITE_NAME} lives in its owner's GitHub account, and only GitHub can take it down:
      send your notice to GitHub, following <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/content-removal-policies/guide-to-submitting-a-dmca-takedown-notice">GitHub's guide</a>
      (at the source, because GitHub holds the code). Report the repository here too, with the reason
      “copyright”: while GitHub decides, the owner may hide it from {SITE_NAME}'s pages, and {SITE_NAME}'s own
      copies of its scripts go with the removal page above.
    </p>

    <h2 id="private">3. Private information</h2>
    <p>
      Someone's address, telephone, health or identity documents published without consent are hidden as soon as
      the owner reads the report; a report for private information may be sent without an account. In a paper's
      record, use the removal page with the reason “personal data”.
    </p>

    <h2 id="counter">4. Counter-notice and restoration</h2>
    <p>
      When something of yours is hidden after a copyright notice, your page <a href="/account/moderation/">“What
      of mine is hidden”</a> offers a counter-notice: say why the content is yours to publish, or was removed by
      mistake, and confirm that you believe so in good faith and that what you say is accurate. The owner then
      restores it, unless the claimant shows within 14 days that they have taken the matter to court. [The
      owner: confirm this delay and the procedure under the law that applies.] Any other decision may be
      appealed once, the same way.
    </p>

    <h2 id="notices">5. Public notices</h2>
    <p>
      Each decision is published on <a href="/notices/">the notices</a>, redacted: what kind of thing, why, when,
      and whether it was restored — never the removed words, never who asked, never private information.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
